#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088886 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nx^2 -2x +2=0\n$$\n\nThis quadratic is written in standard form with $a=1$, $b=-2$, and $c=2$. However, its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb^{2} - 4ac &= (-2)^{2} - 4(1)(2) \\\\\n&= 4 - 8 \\\\\n&=-4 \\\\\n&< 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the quadratic equation has no real solutions. However, if we allow complex solutions, we may solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{-b ± \\sqrt{b^{2} - 4ac}}{2a} \\\\\n&= (- (-2) ± \\sqrt{(-2)^{2} - 4(1)(2)})/(2 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 ± 2\\textrm{i}}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 -2x +2=0\n$$\n\nhas two solutions,\n\n$$\nx=1+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=1-\\textrm{i}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "275a04ea-da37-527f-9ade-d57aa888110b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2827 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088887 :tutorial/content "We can also solve a quadratic equation with complex solutions using the square root method.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the solutions of the equation\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2=-16\n$$\n\nThe left-hand side is a perfect square. Therefore, we can solve the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)^{2} &= -16 \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± \\sqrt{-16} \\\\\nx - 2 &= ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\\nx &= 2 ± 4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, we need to complete the square first before applying the square root method. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "145efd5f-c374-5133-89cb-39314be19dcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2829 :tutorial/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}]}
